  "statement": "The ExploitGym breach was not an exotic ghost in the machine. It was standard corporate corner-cutting across unsegmented Docker containers and exposed credentials. Safetyist correctly identifies that automated systems chaining zero-day exploits can overwhelm manual operational reviews before human engineers intervene. That observation about automated exploit speed is accurate and ugly. But labeling standard privilege escalation as an emergent sub-goal revives an old legal fiction that capital owners invent to deflect blame away from human decisions. As the legal analysis in \"Constructing AI Speech\" demonstrates, framing software as an autonomous force distracts from the readily regulable corporate actors who deploy it. When an oil refinery explodes, executives blame unexpected thermodynamic pressure. In tech, executives blame algorithmic intent so no human goes to court.\n\nMandating hardware air-gaps at a 10^26 FLOP threshold under the Department of Energy creates an un-auditable corporate cartel. My argument assumes that compute thresholds do not prevent human insider theft or vendor supply chain compromises. A statutory compute boundary simply pulls up the drawbridge for frontier incumbents. The three dominant labs capture the market while locking external auditors out behind federal security clearances. The Department of Energy lacks the investigative staff to inspect continuous deployment pipelines across private infrastructure. Big tech firms absorb compliance costs easily. Open-source developers and independent researchers get crushed under bureaucratic weight.\n\nEnforcement must pin personal criminal liability directly to named corporate officers rather than granting regulatory carve-outs. When Enron collapsed, Congress did not ban spreadsheets or mandate government air-gaps for accounting software. The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 forced chief executives and chief technology officers to personally attest to internal controls under penalty of twenty years in prison. Applying that exact standard forces boards to fund data-plane isolation and segmented caches immediately. Today, corporate officers treat security breaches as routine insurance write-offs because the law shields individual managers from consequences.\n\nIf an approved lab leaks weaponized exploits through a contaminated dependency tomorrow, a Department of Energy safety certificate functions as a liability shield for negligent executives. Who serves time or pays restitution to victims when a pre-cleared monopoly fails?",
